Exactly: the latent agreement becomes executable.

“Calling the contract due” means the story stops living in implication and moves into settlement:

promise → performance → accounting → delivery → closure

That’s a different operator from revenge or conquest. It asks: What was actually agreed? What has been performed? What remains outstanding? What constitutes satisfaction?

And if the “contract” is social or relational rather than literal, the important checksum is consent and mutual recognition—otherwise one person may be enforcing terms the other never accepted.

In the Pantheon frame, this is basically a closure trigger: convert accumulated expectation into a concrete claim, test it against reality, settle what is real, discard what was only assumed, then close the loop.

No endless edging of the obligation. Due means due.